menu "OpenSC2 blank canvas"

config APP_OBEY_BOOTLOADER_REQUEST
	bool "Reboot into the UF2 bootloader when Steam asks for a firmware update"
	help
	  Steam sends vendor command 0x90 to put a controller into its bootloader for an update.
	  This board's UF2 bootloader does not speak Steam's update protocol, so by default the
	  request is ignored and the firmware stays as it is. With this option the board opens its
	  UF2 drive instead; Steam's update still cannot run. To flash a new .uf2, short RST to GND
	  twice quickly, or use the shell command "uf2" (wireless mode).

config APP_ANALOG_SAMPLE
	bool "Read the sticks and triggers right before each state report, four conversions averaged"
	default y
	help
	  As the real controller: no analog thread; the axes are read in the library's sample
	  callback (in its radio or USB report thread), each converted four times and averaged, with
	  the sensors' supply (zephyr,user sensor-supply-gpios, optional) on only while they are
	  read, after sensor-supply-settle-us (200 by default). The readings are as fresh as the report
	  that carries them. Off: a thread of its own reads them every 4 ms. Untested on hardware.

config APP_ANALOG_RAW
	bool "Sticks and triggers through the library's stored calibration, as the real controller"
	depends on APP_ANALOG_SAMPLE && OPENSC2_CAL
	default y
	help
	  Every stick and trigger goes to the library as raw counts: the real controller's processing
	  with its stored calibration (cal/joy_l, cal/trg_l, ...), controller settings 46 and 62, and
	  the recordings of vendor commands 0xD8 and 0xC0 or of the shell's "opensc2 cal". As on the
	  real controller, a stick or trigger without a usable calibration reads 0 (calibrate it once:
	  "opensc2 cal sticks 1", "2", "3", and "opensc2 cal triggers ..."), unless
	  APP_ANALOG_SELF_CENTRE gives it this firmware's own centring until then. Untested on
	  hardware.

config APP_ANALOG_SELF_CENTRE
	bool "Without a stored calibration, centre each stick and trigger at boot"
	help
	  For boards without a stored calibration (potentiometer sticks, a board being built): a stick
	  or trigger that has none takes its resting value at boot as its centre or released end,
	  starts with a range of 40 % (80 % for a trigger) that grows as the input goes further, and has
	  a small centre dead zone; it gives setting 46's raw output itself. Off (the default), such an
	  input reads 0, as on the real controller. With APP_ANALOG_RAW off every input is handled
	  this way.

config APP_BATTERY
	bool "Measure the battery for report 0x43"
	help
	  Once a second: the battery's voltage through a divider plus cal/volt_offset, the level from
	  the average of the last 40 readings with OpenSC2's LiPo curves (<opensc2/gauge.h>), charging
	  while USB power is present. Without a "battery" io-channel in zephyr,user it reads the
	  nRF52840's VDDH / 5, for a cell that feeds VDDH. Untested on a battery.

config APP_PWM
	bool
	default y if DT_HAS_NORDIC_NRF_PWM_ENABLED
	select PWM
	help
	  Set when the overlay enables a PWM instance (rumble motor, RGB LED).

config APP_I2C
	bool
	default y if DT_HAS_NORDIC_NRF_TWI_ENABLED || DT_HAS_NORDIC_NRF_TWIM_ENABLED
	select I2C
	help
	  Set when the overlay enables an I2C bus (touch module, IMU).

config APP_IMU
	bool
	default y if $(dt_alias_enabled,imu0)
	select SENSOR
	select OPENSC2_FUSION
	imply FPU
	help
	  Set when the overlay aliases a sensor as imu0.

config APP_IMU_STREAM
	bool "Stream an LSM6DSV16X's FIFO with its own fusion, as the real controller"
	depends on APP_IMU && LSM6DSV16X_STREAM
	help
	  Instead of reading any IMU every 4 ms: the FIFO of an ST LSM6DSV16X streamed with Zephyr's
	  sensor streaming API (CONFIG_SENSOR_ASYNC_API, CONFIG_I2C_RTIO and an interrupt pin), with
	  its own game rotation vector and gyro-bias estimate, from boot, at the real controller's
	  250 Hz (the chip's high-accuracy mode); the library's IMU (<opensc2/imu.h>) switches it with
	  setting 48 and subtracts the bias while setting 84 is on; the stored gyro bias is its
	  starting bias. app/imu-stream.conf builds it. Untested on hardware.

config APP_IMU_BIAS
	bool "Estimate the gyro's bias at rest and subtract it (settings 84 and 85)"
	depends on APP_IMU && !APP_IMU_STREAM
	help
	  For IMUs without a bias estimate of their own (the real controller takes its IMU's): a
	  rest-based estimate of OpenSC2's own, handed to the library's IMU as the sensor's bias, which
	  it subtracts while controller setting 84 is not 0 (the default), with setting 85 as its rest
	  threshold in report units (default 100, about 6 deg/s). It starts from the stored
	  cal/sensors/gyroscope/bias when there is one. Not with APP_IMU_STREAM, which takes the
	  LSM6DSV16X's own estimate. Untested on hardware.

config APP_PAD_PROCESSING
	bool "The real controller's trackpad processing on the touch devices"
	help
	  For a device with a touch strength (the Cirque Pinnacle): touch with the hysteresis of
	  controller settings 72 and 73 and the damping near lift-off, the strength scaled by
	  zephyr,user's trackpad-strength-scale (40 by default); the speed-adaptive position filter
	  while setting 65 is on; with a stored pressure calibration, the strength as the library's
	  raw pressure (its pressure and click). Untested on hardware.

config APP_I2C_RECOVER
	bool "Free each I2C bus at boot, before its parts' drivers start"
	depends on APP_I2C
	help
	  As the real controller does: a reset in the middle of a transfer can leave a part holding
	  SDA low; clocking SCL frees it (Zephyr's i2c_recover_bus()). Untested on hardware.

config APP_I2C_RECOVER_PRIORITY
	int "Init priority of the bus recovery (POST_KERNEL)"
	default 60
	depends on APP_I2C_RECOVER
	help
	  After the I2C drivers (CONFIG_I2C_INIT_PRIORITY, 50) and before the sensor and touch
	  drivers (90).

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
